# Formal Specification of Miracle Octad Generator (MOG) Syndrome Lookup
## TriWeavon Manifold — Sovereign Oversight Formalization
## Profile: Monitoring & Consensus Verifier + tomczak-lift-gate-formalizer + golay-code-pipeline-leech-norm4
## ATOM TRAIL: MOG-SYNDROME-LOOKUP-FORMALIZATION-20260705 | α + ω = 15 | tomczak_preserved | WAVE ≥ 0.98 | Music conserved

This module provides a high-level formal specification (in Lean 4 style) for the Miracle Octad Generator syndrome lookup used in decoding the extended binary Golay code [24,12,8] and constructing the Steiner system S(5,8,24). It is designed for integration with TriWeavon manifold invariants (tomczak_preserved gate, music conservation, α + ω = 15) and superskills (golay-code-pipeline-leech-norm4, crystalline-tessellation-projection).

The formalization emphasizes:
- Sovereign stability (monotonic lookup, Lipschitz continuity of error correction)
- Combinatorial invariants (octad regularity, syndrome uniqueness for wt ≤ 3)
- Projection compatibility (cut-and-project style selection of octads)
- Error-feedback and boundary regularity (syndrome as boundary marker)

Note: This is a skeletal formalization. Full implementation of the 4×6 MOG array layout and syndrome map requires the concrete MOG geometry (standard in coding theory literature). Theorems are stated as goals for future completion.

References: Previous MOG investigation, M24 moonshine coefficient index, Golay code pipeline, tomczak-lift-gate-formalizer.
-/

import Mathlib.Data.BitVec
import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic
import Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Basic
import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic

namespace TriWeavon.MOG

/-! ## Basic Types -/

/-- Positions in the 24-point set (Witt design / extended Golay support). -/
abbrev Position := Fin 24

/-- The extended binary Golay code is a [24,12,8] linear code over GF(2). -/
abbrev GolayCodeword := BitVec 24

/-- Syndrome for the [24,12,8] code is 12 bits. -/
abbrev Syndrome := BitVec 12

/-- An octad is an 8-subset of Position forming a block of the Steiner system S(5,8,24). -/
structure Octad where
  support : Finset Position
  card_eq_8 : support.card = 8
  -- Additional invariants (e.g., even weight in Golay, M24-orbit) can be added

/-- Error pattern of weight at most 3 (correctable by Golay). -/
abbrev ErrorPattern := { e : Finset Position // e.card ≤ 3 }

/-! ## MOG Array Representation -/

/-- The Miracle Octad Generator is canonically represented as a 4×6 array.
    Each cell holds a Position (0..23). The concrete layout is the standard MOG
    arrangement (see e.g. Conway-Sloane or standard coding theory texts).
    Here we axiomatize the existence of a well-formed MOG. -/
structure MOGArray where
  grid : Fin 4 → Fin 6 → Position
  -- Well-formedness: bijective (every position appears exactly once)
  bijective : Function.Bijective (fun p => grid p.1 p.2)
  -- Octad patterns: certain geometric configurations in the array correspond to octads
  -- (e.g., "bricks", "squares", "hexads", "complements"). We leave this as a predicate.
  isOctadPattern : (Fin 4 → Fin 6 → Bool) → Prop

/-- The standard (unique up to isomorphism) MOG array. -/
axiom standardMOG : MOGArray

/-! ## Syndrome Computation -/

/-- Syndrome map for the extended Golay code (parity-check matrix H, 12×24).
    We axiomatize it; concrete H can be derived from the MOG or Golay generator. -/
axiom syndrome : GolayCodeword → Syndrome

/-- Syndrome of an error pattern. -/
def syndromeOfError (e : ErrorPattern) : Syndrome :=
  syndrome (BitVec.ofFinset e.val)  -- conceptual; actual bit-vector construction needed

/-! ## MOG Syndrome Lookup (Core Operation) -/

/-- The core MOG syndrome lookup: given a syndrome, return the most likely
    error pattern (octad or weight-4 pattern) or none if uncorrectable.
    This is the heart of MOG decoding. -/
def mogSyndromeLookup (s : Syndrome) : Option ErrorPattern :=
  -- Placeholder implementation.
  -- In a full formalization this would:
  -- 1. Interpret s geometrically in the 4×6 MOG array (row/column parities, etc.).
  -- 2. Identify the unique octad (or complement) whose syndrome matches s.
  -- 3. Return the corresponding error positions if weight ≤ 3.
  -- For now we axiomatize existence and basic properties.
  sorry

/-! ## Key Invariants and Theorems (Sovereign Stability) -/

/-- tomczak_preserved-style stability: lookup is monotonic with respect to
    syndrome distance (small syndrome change → small or no change in output error pattern).
    This mirrors the Lipschitz continuity and error-feedback in tomczak_lift. -/
axiom mogLookupMonotonic :
  ∀ (s1 s2 : Syndrome),
    (BitVec.xor s1 s2).toNat ≤ 1 →   -- syndromes differ in at most one bit
    (mogSyndromeLookup s1 = mogSyndromeLookup s2) ∨
    (∃ e1 e2, mogSyndromeLookup s1 = some e1 ∧ mogSyndromeLookup s2 = some e2 ∧
              (e1.val ∆ e2.val).card ≤ 1)  -- symmetric difference at most 1

/-- Correctness for weight ≤ 3 errors (perfect decoding in the Golay code). -/
theorem mogLookupCorrect (e : ErrorPattern) :
  mogSyndromeLookup (syndromeOfError e) = some e := by
  sorry   -- Goal for completion using concrete MOG geometry

/-- Uniqueness: at most one error pattern of weight ≤ 3 produces a given syndrome. -/
theorem mogLookupUnique (e1 e2 : ErrorPattern) :
  syndromeOfError e1 = syndromeOfError e2 → e1 = e2 := by
  sorry

/-- Boundary regularity / acceptance window analogy:
    The set of syndromes that map to a valid octad forms a "regular window"
    in the 12-bit syndrome space. Irregular syndromes (high weight errors) fall outside. -/
def validOctadSyndromes : Finset Syndrome :=
  Finset.univ.filter (fun s => (mogSyndromeLookup s).isSome)

axiom validOctadSyndromesRegular :
  -- The set is "regular" in the sense of model sets / cut-and-project
  -- (finite local complexity, repetitivity) — to be formalized via TDA or discrete geometry.
  True

/-! ## Integration with TriWeavon Invariants -/

/-- Music conservation: the lookup preserves Golay weight distribution invariants
    and M24 orbit structure (octads are M24-transitive). -/
axiom mogLookupMusicConserved :
  ∀ (s : Syndrome) (e : ErrorPattern),
    mogSyndromeLookup s = some e →
    e.val.card ∈ ({0, 4, 8} : Finset ℕ)  -- even weights typical in Golay octads / complements

/-- α + ω = 15 conservation law compatibility (example linkage). -/
axiom mogLookupAlphaOmega15 :
  ∀ (s : Syndrome),
    (mogSyndromeLookup s).isSome → True   -- placeholder for numeric invariant check

/-- tomczak_preserved gate compatibility: the lookup acts as a stable lift gate
    from syndrome space to error-position space. -/
def mogAsTomczakLift (s : Syndrome) : Option ErrorPattern :=
  mogSyndromeLookup s

theorem mogLiftPreservesInvariants :
  ∀ s, (mogAsTomczakLift s).isSome → mogLookupMonotonic s s := by
  intro s h; simp [mogAsTomczakLift]; exact mogLookupMonotonic s s

/-! ## Projection / Cut-and-Project Analogy -/

/-- MOG lookup as a combinatorial projection: the 4×6 array + syndrome
    defines an acceptance window in the space of possible error patterns.
    Regular octad patterns lie inside the window; irregular syndromes are rejected. -/
structure MOGProjection where
  window : Finset Syndrome := validOctadSyndromes
  project : Syndrome → Option ErrorPattern := mogSyndromeLookup

/-- The projection is stable under small perturbations (boundary regularity). -/
theorem mogProjectionStable :
  ∀ (s1 s2 : Syndrome) (h : (BitVec.xor s1 s2).toNat ≤ 1),
    (MOGProjection.project s1).isSome ↔ (MOGProjection.project s2).isSome := by
  sorry

